---
import Annex from '../../layouts/Annex.astro';

// Interactive cutoff figure: neighbor layout computed at build time.
const CX = 250, CY = 190, SCALE = 16; // pixels per ångström
const DEFAULT_RC = 6;
const NBRS = [
  { d: 2.3, a: 18 }, { d: 2.7, a: 152 }, { d: 3.1, a: 268 }, { d: 3.5, a: 74 },
  { d: 3.9, a: 205 }, { d: 4.2, a: 332 }, { d: 4.6, a: 112 }, { d: 5.0, a: 12 },
  { d: 5.3, a: 242 }, { d: 5.7, a: 300 }, { d: 6.1, a: 176 }, { d: 6.4, a: 44 },
  { d: 6.8, a: 128 }, { d: 7.1, a: 210 }, { d: 7.4, a: 350 }, { d: 7.8, a: 88 },
  { d: 8.2, a: 268 }, { d: 8.6, a: 158 }, { d: 9.0, a: 28 }, { d: 9.4, a: 228 },
].map((n) => ({
  ...n,
  x: +(CX + n.d * SCALE * Math.cos((n.a * Math.PI) / 180)).toFixed(1),
  y: +(CY + n.d * SCALE * Math.sin((n.a * Math.PI) / 180)).toFixed(1),
}));
---
<Annex
  title="The Machine"
  piece="01"
  eyebrow="Builder's Annex · 01 · Understand"
  dek="How a pile of atoms becomes an energy and a force, and the five properties of that computation that shape everything you serve, speed up, or fine-tune."
  nextHref="/annex/the-performance"
  nextLabel="Where the Time Goes"
>
  <p>
    A machine-learned interatomic potential is a function. Atoms go in, each with an atomic number
    and a position, and one number comes out: the energy of that arrangement, in electron-volts.
    The force on each atom is the gradient of that number, how fast the energy changes as the atom
    moves, and forces are what a simulation actually consumes. The output you care about is a
    derivative of the output the model computes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The function is called inside a loop. Molecular dynamics reads the forces, moves every atom by a
    femtosecond, and calls again, a million times over; a relaxation walks the atoms downhill until
    the forces vanish. Nothing else runs between calls, so the model is the whole workload, and its
    shape is not the shape of the model-serving you are used to.
  </p>

  <p>
    This piece builds the function from the outside in, how a pile of atoms becomes an energy and
    how the forces fall out, and then names the five properties of that computation that shape
    everything you serve, speed up, or fine-tune. Where a comparison to transformers helps, it is
    here in a line, and you can skip it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The training labels come from density functional theory, accurate and slow at CPU-hours a
    snapshot. The model is a fit to it, three to six orders of magnitude faster. That is the pitch.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the atoms</h2>

  <p>
    Each atom starts as a lookup. Its atomic number (1 for hydrogen, 8 for oxygen, 79 for gold)
    indexes a learned table with about a hundred rows, one per element, producing an initial feature
    vector of shape <code>[N, k]</code>. That is the entire use of an atom's identity, and it carries
    almost nothing: two carbon atoms are interchangeable. Everything that distinguishes one carbon
    from another is in the positions, which are continuous and move every step. (If you know
    transformers: the element embedding is the token embedding, but here identity is a hundred-way
    choice and the signal lives in geometry, not the other way around.)
  </p>

  <h2>Locality: each atom sees only its neighbors</h2>

  <p>
    The total energy is a sum of per-atom contributions,
  </p>

  <pre><code>E = ε₁ + ε₂ + ... + ε_N</code></pre>

  <p>
    and each atom's contribution depends only on neighbors within a cutoff radius, 5 to 6 ångströms.
    Interactions fall off fast with distance, so the atoms that set a carbon's energy sit a bond or
    two away, not across the box. This is the bet that makes the whole thing cheap and scalable, and
    it holds for neutral bulk matter. Ionic and interfacial systems carry real
    electrostatics past any cutoff, and the closing section returns to them.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig" id="cutoff-fig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 400" role="img" aria-label="A central atom with a cutoff sphere whose radius is set by a slider. Neighbors inside the sphere are joined to it by edges; atoms outside are faint. As the radius grows, the number of neighbors inside grows as the cube of the radius.">
        <circle id="cut-circle" cx={CX} cy={CY} r={DEFAULT_RC * SCALE} class="s-acc f-wash" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4" />
        <line id="cut-rline" x1={CX} y1={CY} x2={CX + DEFAULT_RC * SCALE} y2={CY} class="s-acc" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
        <text id="cut-rlabel" x={CX + (DEFAULT_RC * SCALE) / 2} y={CY - 8} class="t-xs t-acc" text-anchor="middle">rᶜ = {DEFAULT_RC.toFixed(1)} Å</text>
        {NBRS.map((n) => (
          <g class:list={["nbr", n.d > DEFAULT_RC && "out"]} data-d={n.d}>
            <line x1={CX} y1={CY} x2={n.x} y2={n.y} />
            <circle cx={n.x} cy={n.y} r="6" />
          </g>
        ))}
        <circle cx={CX} cy={CY} r="9" class="f-acc" />
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-controls">
      <span class="fig-ctl-label">cutoff</span>
      <input type="range" min="2.5" max="8" step="0.1" value="6" class="fig-slider" aria-label="cutoff radius in ångströms" />
      <span class="fig-readout" id="cut-readout">6.0 Å · ≈ 90 neighbors/atom · ≈ 450k edges</span>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 1 · drag it</b> The cutoff fixes who interacts with whom, set by geometry rather than learned, and a neighbor-list pass writes the pairs as an <code>edge_index</code> of shape <code>[2, E]</code>. Neighbor count grows as the <em>cube</em> of the radius: at 6 Å, liquid water holds about 90 neighbors an atom, so a 5,000-atom box already carries ~450,000 directed edges. Every extra ångström of cutoff buys a lot more compute, which is why the choice is a real one.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var fig = document.getElementById("cutoff-fig");
      if (!fig) return;
      var CX = 250, CY = 190, SCALE = 16, DENS = 0.1, NATOMS = 5000;
      var slider = fig.querySelector(".fig-slider");
      var circle = fig.querySelector("#cut-circle");
      var rline = fig.querySelector("#cut-rline");
      var rlabel = fig.querySelector("#cut-rlabel");
      var readout = fig.querySelector("#cut-readout");
      var nbrs = Array.prototype.slice.call(fig.querySelectorAll(".nbr"));
      function fmtEdges(e) {
        return e >= 1e6 ? (e / 1e6).toFixed(1) + "M" : Math.round(e / 1000) + "k";
      }
      function update() {
        var rA = parseFloat(slider.value);
        var rpx = rA * SCALE;
        circle.setAttribute("r", rpx);
        rline.setAttribute("x2", CX + rpx);
        rlabel.setAttribute("x", CX + rpx / 2);
        rlabel.textContent = "rᶜ = " + rA.toFixed(1) + " Å";
        nbrs.forEach(function (g) {
          g.classList.toggle("out", parseFloat(g.getAttribute("data-d")) > rA);
        });
        var nn = Math.round((DENS * 4 / 3) * Math.PI * rA * rA * rA);
        readout.textContent =
          rA.toFixed(1) + " Å · ≈ " + nn + " neighbors/atom · ≈ " + fmtEdges(nn * NATOMS) + " edges";
      }
      slider.addEventListener("input", update);
      update();
    })();
  </script>

  <p>
    The list is built cheaply. Measuring all <em>N</em>²/2 distances is quadratic; cell lists make
    it linear by binning atoms into cutoff-wide cubes and searching only the 27-cube shell around
    each atom. The output is an edge list, the adjacency of a graph, which any graph code already
    knows how to hold.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each edge then becomes features. A transformer tags every token with where it sits in the
    sequence, its positional encoding, so the network can use order; the MLIP equivalent tags each
    edge with the geometry between two atoms. Its length and its direction are handled separately.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 340" role="img" aria-label="One edge vector split into two channels: its scalar length expanded over a bank of radial basis bumps, and its unit direction evaluated on spherical harmonics of order 0 through 3.">
        <!-- the edge -->
        <circle cx="70" cy="80" r="8" class="f-acc" />
        <circle cx="196" cy="140" r="6" class="f-ink" />
        <line x1="78" y1="86" x2="188" y2="135" class="s-ink" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#m2a)" />
        <text x="86" y="76" class="t-xs t-lab">edge vector</text>
        <text x="132" y="126" class="t-xs t-lab">r</text>

        <line x1="228" y1="110" x2="270" y2="110" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#m2b)" />

        <!-- branch A: distance -> radial basis -->
        <text x="292" y="60" class="t-s t-acc">length ‖r‖ → radial basis</text>
        <g class="s-acc" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none">
          <path d="M292 132 q16 -34 32 0" />
          <path d="M320 132 q16 -30 32 0" />
          <path d="M348 132 q16 -26 32 0" />
          <path d="M376 132 q16 -22 32 0" />
          <path d="M404 132 q16 -18 32 0" />
        </g>
        <line x1="286" y1="132" x2="452" y2="132" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="292" y="150" class="t-xs t-lab">a soft one-hot over distance, [E, 8]</text>

        <!-- branch B: direction -> spherical harmonics -->
        <text x="292" y="212" class="t-s t-acc">direction r̂ → spherical harmonics</text>
        <!-- l=0 -->
        <circle cx="312" cy="262" r="12" class="f-panel s-ink" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="312" y="298" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">l=0</text>
        <text x="312" y="312" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">1</text>
        <!-- l=1 dumbbell -->
        <g transform="translate(372,262)"><circle cx="0" cy="-11" r="8" class="f-acc" opacity="0.85"/><circle cx="0" cy="11" r="8" class="f-ink" opacity="0.55"/></g>
        <text x="372" y="298" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">l=1</text>
        <text x="372" y="312" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">3</text>
        <!-- l=2 cloverleaf -->
        <g transform="translate(432,262)" class="f-acc" opacity="0.8"><circle cx="-9" cy="-9" r="6"/><circle cx="9" cy="-9" r="6"/><circle cx="-9" cy="9" r="6"/><circle cx="9" cy="9" r="6"/></g>
        <text x="432" y="298" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">l=2</text>
        <text x="432" y="312" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">5</text>
        <!-- l=3 -->
        <g transform="translate(492,262)" class="f-acc" opacity="0.75"><circle cx="0" cy="-11" r="5"/><circle cx="-11" cy="-2" r="5"/><circle cx="11" cy="-2" r="5"/><circle cx="-6" cy="10" r="5"/><circle cx="6" cy="10" r="5"/></g>
        <text x="492" y="298" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">l=3</text>
        <text x="492" y="312" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">7</text>

        <text x="560" y="256" class="t-s t-ink">1+3+5+7</text>
        <text x="560" y="274" class="t-s t-ink">= 16</text>
        <text x="560" y="292" class="t-xs t-lab">= (l_max+1)²</text>
        <text x="560" y="310" class="t-xs t-lab">per edge</text>

        <defs>
          <marker id="m2a" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="f-ink" /></marker>
          <marker id="m2b" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--rule)" /></marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 2</b> An edge is a relative position: how far and in which direction the neighbor sits, never an absolute slot in a sequence, because physics does not care where the origin is. The length expands over a bank of radial bumps; the direction evaluates on spherical harmonics, whose multiplicities run 1, 3, 5, 7 as the order climbs. The <code>l=1</code> triple is just the components of the unit vector. Everything the model knows about geometry enters here.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Message passing: atoms update from their neighbors</h2>

  <p>
    Each atom now has a starting feature vector and a list of neighbors. A message-passing block
    updates every atom from those neighbors, and it burns most of the compute. One round has four
    steps.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-label="One round of message passing: neighbors send messages along edges into a center atom, the messages are summed, and the center atom's feature vector is updated.">
        <!-- graph -->
        <g class="s-rule" stroke-width="1.2">
          <line x1="150" y1="128" x2="70" y2="70" />
          <line x1="150" y1="128" x2="80" y2="196" />
          <line x1="150" y1="128" x2="238" y2="72" />
          <line x1="150" y1="128" x2="228" y2="190" />
        </g>
        <g class="s-acc" stroke-width="1.6">
          <line x1="78" y1="76" x2="138" y2="120" marker-end="url(#m3)" />
          <line x1="88" y1="190" x2="140" y2="140" marker-end="url(#m3)" />
          <line x1="230" y1="78" x2="162" y2="120" marker-end="url(#m3)" />
          <line x1="220" y1="184" x2="162" y2="138" marker-end="url(#m3)" />
        </g>
        <g class="f-paper s-ink" stroke-width="1.4">
          <circle cx="70" cy="70" r="8" /><circle cx="80" cy="196" r="8" />
          <circle cx="238" cy="72" r="8" /><circle cx="228" cy="190" r="8" />
        </g>
        <circle cx="150" cy="128" r="10" class="f-acc" />
        <text x="150" y="228" class="t-s t-lab" text-anchor="middle">4 neighbors → 1 center</text>

        <!-- pipeline -->
        <g transform="translate(316,0)">
          <text x="0" y="60" class="t-s"><tspan class="t-acc">1 gather</tspan>  neighbor feats onto edges  [E, k]</text>
          <text x="0" y="96" class="t-s"><tspan class="t-acc">2 tensor product</tspan>  feats ⊗ Yₗ, gated by radial  [E, k′]</text>
          <text x="0" y="132" class="t-s"><tspan class="t-acc">3 scatter-add</tspan>  sum messages onto atom  [N, k′]</text>
          <text x="0" y="168" class="t-s"><tspan class="t-acc">4 update</tspan>  per-atom MLP  [N, k]</text>
          <line x1="0" y1="188" x2="392" y2="188" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
          <text x="0" y="208" class="t-xs t-lab">gather + scatter are the memory-traffic backbone</text>
        </g>
        <defs>
          <marker id="m3" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="f-acc" /></marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 3</b> Edge-wise compute, aggregate, node-wise update. The sum at step 3 is not a stylistic choice. It keeps the result unchanged when you reorder an atom's neighbors, which it must be, since atoms have no order.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Depth buys reach. After one round an atom has seen 6 Å
    out. At the second round its neighbors are already carrying summaries of <em>their</em>
    neighborhoods, so information arrives from 12 Å.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 170" role="img" aria-label="A chain of atoms drawn three times. After each round of message passing, the accent-colored span of atoms the center has heard from grows one hop wider.">
        <g>
          <text x="120" y="34" class="t-s t-lab" text-anchor="middle">after 1 round · 6 Å</text>
          <line x1="40" y1="86" x2="200" y2="86" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
          <line x1="96" y1="86" x2="144" y2="86" class="s-acc" stroke-width="2.6" />
          <g class="f-ink"><circle cx="48" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="72" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="96" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="144" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="168" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="192" cy="86" r="4"/></g>
          <circle cx="120" cy="86" r="5.5" class="f-acc" />
        </g>
        <g>
          <text x="360" y="34" class="t-s t-lab" text-anchor="middle">after 2 rounds · 12 Å</text>
          <line x1="280" y1="86" x2="440" y2="86" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
          <line x1="312" y1="86" x2="408" y2="86" class="s-acc" stroke-width="2.6" />
          <g class="f-ink"><circle cx="288" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="312" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="336" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="384" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="408" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="432" cy="86" r="4"/></g>
          <circle cx="360" cy="86" r="5.5" class="f-acc" />
        </g>
        <g>
          <text x="600" y="34" class="t-s t-lab" text-anchor="middle">after 3 rounds · 18 Å</text>
          <line x1="516" y1="86" x2="684" y2="86" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
          <line x1="528" y1="86" x2="672" y2="86" class="s-acc" stroke-width="2.6" />
          <g class="f-ink"><circle cx="528" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="552" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="576" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="624" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="648" cy="86" r="4"/><circle cx="672" cy="86" r="4"/></g>
          <circle cx="600" cy="86" r="5.5" class="f-acc" />
        </g>
        <text x="360" y="140" class="t-s t-ink" text-anchor="middle">receptive field = rounds × cutoff</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 4</b> The receptive field grows one hop a round. Notice that the model never draws an edge longer than the cutoff; range comes from stacking, not from reaching. This is also the multi-node cost, since a domain must exchange ghost atoms out to the full receptive field.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The family tree is variations on this block, and it reads as a genealogy. SchNet (2017) sends
    distances only, invariant scalars, no directions. NequIP answered its blind spot by making the
    features equivariant, the property the third section takes up. MACE answered NequIP's cost: it builds
    many-body products inside each layer, so one layer captures three and four atoms at once ("two
    neighbors 104 degrees apart," a water molecule's H–O–H angle) and it stops at two layers.
    Allegro drops message passing entirely; every atom's energy reads only its own neighborhood, at
    one cutoff, no matter the depth. That looks like a ceiling. The multi-node section makes it the
    selling point.
  </p>

  <h2>The readout: per-atom energies, summed</h2>

  <p>
    After the last block, the scalar (<code>l=0</code>) channels of each atom pass through a small
    readout MLP to one number, that atom's energy. Sum over atoms for the total. The sum is not a
    detail: it hard-codes extensivity, so two separated copies of a system have exactly twice the
    energy. Averaging would break that. Summing cannot.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-label="A raw DFT energy drawn as a tall bar that is almost entirely per-element reference baseline, with a thin slice at the top marking the learned residual. A second bar on a mismatched baseline shows a silent constant offset.">
        <!-- bar 1 -->
        <rect x="120" y="40" width="90" height="150" class="f-panel s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
        <rect x="120" y="40" width="90" height="20" class="f-acc" />
        <text x="165" y="210" class="t-s t-lab" text-anchor="middle">model A</text>
        <line x1="222" y1="40" x2="250" y2="40" class="s-acc" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="256" y="44" class="t-xs t-acc">learned residual (the chemistry)</text>
        <line x1="222" y1="120" x2="250" y2="120" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="256" y="124" class="t-xs t-lab">per-element reference energy</text>
        <text x="256" y="138" class="t-xs t-lab">(isolated-atom baseline)</text>

        <!-- bar 2 mismatched -->
        <rect x="470" y="72" width="90" height="150" class="f-panel s-rule" stroke-width="1" />
        <rect x="470" y="72" width="90" height="20" class="f-acc" />
        <text x="515" y="210" style="display:none"></text>
        <text x="515" y="242" class="t-s t-lab" text-anchor="middle">model B, other DFT recipe</text>
        <line x1="470" y1="40" x2="470" y2="72" class="s-acc" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        <text x="382" y="58" class="t-xs t-acc">silent</text>
        <text x="382" y="72" class="t-xs t-acc">offset Δ</text>
        <line x1="360" y1="40" x2="620" y2="40" class="s-rule" stroke-width="0.75" stroke-dasharray="2 4" />
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 5</b> One number in the sum dwarfs the rest: each element's reference energy, roughly the atom alone in vacuum. The network only ever fits the thin slice on top. That baseline depends on the DFT recipe, so mixing a fine-tuning set computed at another level shifts every energy by a constant per element. Forces have zero gradient to a constant, so they look fine while every energy comparison is wrong. The fine-tuning piece has the fix.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The training economics rhyme with model work you have seen. Labels cost DFT compute instead of
    annotation, so the field pretrains big on pooled public data and fine-tunes on the niche. A
    single-material potential runs 10⁵ to 10⁶ parameters. MACE-MP-0 medium is 4.7M. UMA-medium
    reaches 1.4 billion parameters total but runs only about 50 million on any given structure. It
    holds several sets of weights, its experts, and a small router picks which to use per input, so
    total capacity grows without every parameter running every time. UMA chooses its experts once
    per structure, from the dataset, task, charge, and spin, and blends them into one weight matrix
    before the forward pass. It is trained across five FAIR datasets, roughly 500 million structures.
  </p>

  <h2>Forces come out of the backward pass</h2>

  <p>
    The forward pass produces the energy. A simulation wants the forces, and in most MLIPs the
    forces are not a second output head. They are the gradient of the energy with respect to the
    input positions, taken by autograd: run the network forward for the energy, then backward for
    the force on every atom.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-label="Positions flow forward through the network to a single scalar energy; the forces come back out of a backward pass differentiating that scalar with respect to the positions. The whole thing repeats every molecular-dynamics step.">
        <text x="70" y="96" class="t-s t-ink">positions</text>
        <text x="70" y="114" class="t-xs t-lab">R  [N,3]</text>
        <line x1="140" y1="100" x2="196" y2="100" class="s-ink" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#m6f)" />
        <rect x="200" y="72" width="150" height="56" class="f-panel s-ink" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="275" y="98" class="t-s t-ink" text-anchor="middle">the network</text>
        <text x="275" y="114" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">forward</text>
        <line x1="354" y1="100" x2="410" y2="100" class="s-ink" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#m6f)" />
        <text x="452" y="96" class="t-s t-ink" text-anchor="middle">E</text>
        <text x="452" y="114" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">scalar</text>

        <!-- backward -->
        <path d="M452 128 C 452 180, 275 180, 275 134" fill="none" class="s-acc" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#m6b)" />
        <text x="364" y="196" class="t-s t-acc" text-anchor="middle">backward: F = −∂E/∂R   [N,3]</text>

        <!-- loop -->
        <path d="M240 60 C 240 20, 500 20, 500 84" fill="none" class="s-rule" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 3" marker-end="url(#m6g)" />
        <text x="370" y="34" class="t-xs t-lab" text-anchor="middle">every MD step, a million times</text>

        <defs>
          <marker id="m6f" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="f-ink" /></marker>
          <marker id="m6b" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" class="f-acc" /></marker>
