    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 6</b> Every production inference is a forward pass <em>and</em> a backward pass. There is no <code>torch.no_grad()</code>. Positions require gradients, the backward graph is built and traversed every step, and it holds the forward activations to differentiate through, so the memory high-water mark of "inference" is training-shaped.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    That completes the machine: atoms to a graph, features on the edges, a few rounds of message
    passing, a per-atom energy summed to a scalar, and the forces as its gradient. Five properties
    of that computation shape everything you build around it.
  </p>

  <h2>1 · Inference is a forward pass and a backward pass</h2>

  <p>
    Because the force is the gradient of the energy, every production call runs the network forward
    for the energy and backward for the forces. There is no <code>torch.no_grad()</code>, and no
    forward-only fast path to reach for. The backward graph is built and traversed on every step,
    holding the forward activations to differentiate through, so the memory high-water mark of an
    inference call looks like training rather than serving. A latency or memory estimate made for a
    forward-only model of the same size will be low. Size for the backward pass.
  </p>

  <p>
    Training pays for the gradient twice. The force is already a first derivative, so a loss on the
    forces, backpropagated to the weights, differentiates a gradient: the double-backward path, at
    roughly 2 to 3 times the memory and time of energy-only training. A fine-tuning job that OOMs at
    a batch size that should fit is usually hitting this. The fine-tuning piece takes the rest of
    that story.
  </p>

  <p>
    Forces taken this way are worth the cost. A force read as the gradient of a scalar is
    conservative: the work around any closed loop of positions is zero, so energy is conserved up to
    integrator error. A direct force head skips the backward pass and predicts three numbers an atom
    at about half the cost. Orb-v3 ships direct and conservative variants; released UMA and eSEN
    train a direct head internally, then remove it and ship conservative. An arbitrary vector field
    is not the gradient of anything, so its discrepancy acts like a hidden thermostat and the energy
    drifts over a long run. I would keep conservative forces for any run you integrate past a few
    picoseconds, and reach for a direct head only for single-point or short-relaxation throughput.
    Your serving stack has to carry both.
  </p>

  <h2>2 · It runs in a loop, not a request stream</h2>

  <p>
    The model's output feeds its next input: forces move the atoms, the moved atoms are the next
    call. Millions of calls in a row, each depending on the one before, and nothing between them but
    a cheap position update. This looks like a workload made for caching, because the atoms barely
    move. One femtosecond of thermal motion shifts an atom by thousandths of an ångström against a 5
    Å cutoff, so the graph and the activations are nearly identical to last step's. The field mostly
    does not cache it.
  </p>

  <p>
    One amortization is universal and old: the neighbor list is built with a skin margin and reused
    until an atom drifts half the skin, which spreads graph construction over many steps. Nothing
    amortizes the network itself. The obstacle is real. Every atom moves every step, and the output
    is a gradient, the quantity least tolerant of a stale approximation, since MD integrates force
    error into drift. Whether an incremental scheme can beat honest recomputation is open. I would
    not build a roadmap on it. What you can lean on is the reverse: throughput comes from running
    many independent trajectories at once, never from making one trajectory's steps cheaper to chain.
  </p>

  <h2>3 · The hidden state carries geometry</h2>

  <p>
    In most networks a hidden state is an unstructured vector, and any layer that maps vectors to
    vectors is fair game. An equivariant MLIP's hidden state is typed. Rotate the input molecule and
    the internal features rotate in lockstep, so the predicted forces come out rotated by exactly the
    same amount. The architecture enforces this. It is not learned from augmentation.
  </p>

