    <figcaption class="afig-cap"><b>Fig 7</b> Each atom's feature is a bundle of typed pieces (irreps): <code>l=0</code> scalars that ignore rotation, <code>l=1</code> vectors that rotate like a direction, higher pieces with their own rules. Combining two types into a third follows the fixed Clebsch–Gordan algebra, and that tensor product, run over every edge, is the hot kernel cuEquivariance exists to fuse. It is the only bilinear op that mixes geometry into features while keeping every channel's rotation type honest.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The consequence: you cannot drop in an arbitrary layer. LayerNorm across a vector's three
    components breaks the rotation rule. An elementwise ReLU on them breaks it. Standard dropout
    breaks it. Equivariant models put nonlinearities on scalar channels only and let scalars gate
    the magnitudes of the higher pieces. If you build fusion passes, quantization, pruning, or
    adapter injection, the typed structure is a correctness constraint, and violating it throws no
    error. It ships a model whose forces silently depend on how the molecule happens to be oriented
    in space.
  </p>

  <h2>4 · Tiny model, enormous input</h2>

  <p>
    Weight-loading bandwidth, the thing serving a large language model fights, is a non-issue here.
    The whole model fits in a few tens of megabytes. Where the memory in one call actually goes is
    the edges.
  </p>

