  <p>
    So <code>torch.compile</code>, CUDA graphs, and kernel fusion matter more than raw FLOPs
    suggest. Batching concatenates several systems into one big disconnected graph and tracks which
    atom belongs to which. The dynamic shapes hurt most: atom count is fixed within a run, edge
    count is not, and a shape that changes every few steps forces recompilation or graph recapture,
    either of which can eat the whole speedup. Padding to a few bucket sizes fixes it. It is the same
    game you play batching variable-length inputs, now per-step, inside a hot loop, forever.
  </p>

  <h2>5 · Correctness is physical, not a number</h2>

  <p>
    A low error on a held-out test set is necessary and not sufficient. The acceptance test is
    behavioral: run constant-energy dynamics for millions of steps and watch the total energy.
    Correlated force errors do not average out. They accumulate, and a simulation that slowly heats
    itself is not slightly wrong. Sometimes it detonates, atoms flying apart mid-run.
  </p>

  <figure class="afig">
    <div class="afig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 230" role="img" aria-label="Total energy versus simulation time. A conservative force field holds a flat line. A direct-force model drifts steadily upward, and its static force error can be lower than the conservative model's.">
