  </figure>

  <p>
    This is why the field runs fp32 and treats mixed precision warily. TF32 matmuls, on by default
    since Ampere, inject the low-amplitude force noise long MD is sensitive to; turn them off before
    you benchmark accuracy. Tight relaxations chasing force norms toward zero sometimes want fp64.
  </p>

  <h2>Two facts the five properties don't cover</h2>

  <p>
    Two more things will bite a team building both inference and fine-tuning tooling, and neither
    falls out of the machine as drawn.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The inference fast path and the fine-tuning path conflict.</strong> Inference speed
    comes from cuEquivariance's fused tensor-product kernels under <code>torch.compile</code> or
    CUDA-graph capture. Force-label fine-tuning needs the double-backward, since you cannot train on
    forces without differentiating a gradient. They do not compose: the fused, compiled path does
    not support the second-order backward (NVIDIA's cuEquivariance issue #268), so the kernels that
    make inference fast get dropped or run eager for fine-tuning. Stand up both against the same
    checkpoints and you maintain two paths through the hot kernel.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Short-range MLIPs miss long-range electrostatics by default.</strong> The locality bet
    loses where charge does not stay local. A 6 Å cutoff cannot see the Coulomb interaction between
    two ions 20 Å apart, and for ionic or interfacial systems that is real physics. The
    patches exist: latent Ewald summation, PME-style message passing carrying a long-range term
    beside the short-range graph. UMA's total-charge and spin inputs let it condition on global
    charge state, which is not the same as summing the electrostatics; a short-range backbone still
    needs the long-range term bolted on to get an interface right.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means for the tools you build</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Serving.</strong> The interfaces are the ASE <code>Calculator</code>
    (<code>get_potential_energy</code>, <code>get_forces</code>) and LAMMPS pair styles, and the
    workload is a hot loop, not a request stream. Latency per call is the metric. Steps cannot batch
    across time, since each consumes the last one's output, so throughput comes from running many
    simulations at once. Provision for forward and backward on every call, plus the direct-force
    fallback for users who choose speed over conservation.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Multi-node.</strong> Big systems split across GPUs by spatial domain, and each domain
    holds ghost copies of neighboring atoms out to the receptive field (Fig 4). Rounds times cutoff
    sets the halo width, so more rounds means more communication. Strictly-local Allegro scales
    across nodes where deep message-passers stall, which makes "how many rounds" an infrastructure
    question, not only an accuracy one.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Speed.</strong> The wins are in kernels and shape discipline: fused equivariant tensor
    products, bucket-padded compilation, neighbor-list tuning. The step-to-step almost-cache is
    still unclaimed, with the obstacle from the loop section in its way. And the fast path fights the
    training path, so budget for both.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the machine. Its five properties are why serving these models, speeding them up, and
    fine-tuning them is a distinct engineering job and not a config change. The next two pieces are
    that job.
  </p>
</Annex>
